  "state": {
    "case_id": "99",
    "case_summary": "A 49-year-old woman with end-stage renal disease due to diabetes, who had been on continuous ambulatory peritoneal dialysis for 22 months, underwent a live-unrelated kidney transplantation in April 2006. For the first six months post-transplantation, her clinical course was stable without any rejection episodes. She was on a regimen of cyclosporine (4 mg/kg/day), mycophenolate mofetil (2 g/day), and prednisolone (5 mg/day). Five months post-transplantation, she developed cellulitis in her right leg following minor trauma and was treated with intravenous cefazolin (4 g/day) and ceftriaxone (2 g/day). Although the cellulitis symptoms initially improved, three weeks later, she developed multiple painful, erythematous, firm nodules ranging from 2 to 7 cm in diameter on the anterior and posterior aspects of her right leg and lower right thigh, with some nodules becoming ulcerated. The patient had a fever of 39°C, blood pressure of 130/90 mmHg, and a regular pulse rate of 80/min. There was no edema or lymphadenopathy, and her systemic examination was unremarkable. Laboratory tests revealed a hemoglobin level of 8.6 g/dL, a total white cell count of 100,000/mm3, and an ESR of 125 mm for the first hour. Serum biochemistry showed a creatinine level of 1.1 mg/dL, fasting blood sugar of 191 mg/dL, uric acid of 2.6 mg/dL, and lactate dehydrogenase of 625 IU/L. She tested negative for cytomegalovirus (CMV) IgM but positive for CMV IgG, consistent with her pretransplantation status. A chest X-ray was unremarkable. MRI of the right leg indicated severe thickening of the superficial soft tissue and skin, with no involvement of deep soft tissue or muscular structures.\n\nHistological examination of deep incisional biopsies of some of the nodules revealed lobular panniculitis with infiltrating lymphocytes, neutrophils, multinucleated giant cells, foamy macrophages, fat necrosis and granulation tissue. Hematoxylin-eosin (H&E) and periodic acid schiff (PAS) staining showed numerous broad, aseptate and irregularly branched fungal hyphae deposited within the hypoderm and vessel wall.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
